<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greek Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greek Civil War was fought from 1946 to 1949 between the government army, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the communist-led Democratic Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. It grew out of conflict between the resistance movement EAM-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a> and the returning government in 1944. Tens of thousands died before the communists were defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grammos">Grammos</a> and Vitsi mountains in 1949, leaving deep divisions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greek-civil-war.jpg" alt="Greek Civil War" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vladina_edinica,_Gradjanska_vojna_vo_Grcija.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> forces approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in April 1941, King George II and his government escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the Germans set up a collaborationist government in Athens. The power vacuum was filled by resistance movements that ranged from monarchist to Communist. The largest was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-greece">National Liberation Front</a> (EAM), founded on 27 September 1941 and controlled by the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> (KKE); its military wing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a>, was founded in February 1942. From mid-1943 the animosity between ELAS and the other movements, such as EDES, erupted into armed conflict. In 1944 ELAS had more than 50,000 fighters, while EDES and EKKA had around 10,000 each.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 1 December 1944 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou">Georgios Papandreou</a> ordered the disarmament of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> forces by 10 December. EAM called a strike and a demonstration on 3 December, in which at least 200,000 people marched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Shooting began near the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier, and more than 28 demonstrators were killed and 148 injured. This began the Dekemvriana, 37 days of full-scale fighting in Athens between EAM fighters and the forces of the British army and the government. The Treaty of Varkiza of February 1945 provided for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a> to be demobilised, an amnesty for political offences, a referendum on the monarchy and a general election.</p><p>Fighting resumed in March 1946, when about 30 former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a> members attacked a police station at Litochoro the night before the elections. The rebels organised as the Democratic Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> (DSE), with Markos Vafiadis in command from a base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>; Yugoslavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> supported them, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> remained ambivalent. About 30,000 children were forcibly taken by the DSE to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> countries, while the Communist leadership said they were evacuated at the request of parents. The royal government was funded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truman-doctrine">Truman Doctrine</a> of 1947 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> of 1948.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The insurgents were demoralised by the split between Stalin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> in June 1948. Party secretary Nikolaos Zachariadis chose to follow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and replaced Vafiadis in January 1949, and in July 1949 Tito closed the Yugoslav border to the DSE. The Hellenic Army under Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandros-papagos">Alexandros Papagos</a> defeated the DSE in its final stages. At least 80,000 people died in the war, and thousands were imprisoned or exiled to the islands of Gyaros and Makronisos, while many others fled to Communist countries or emigrated.</p><p>The KKE remained banned until 1974, and the conflict was officially recognised as a civil war in 1989. The division it left in Greek society contributed to the polarisation of the 1960s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Greek Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Cold War the <b>Greek Civil War</b> and the Truman Doctrine of 1947 made Greece an early front of American containment policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Greece, partisans who had fought the German invasion of 1941 made their own version, &quot;The Hymn of EAM&quot;, with words by Vassilis Rotas, in the years of the <b>Greek Civil War</b> (1946–1949).</blockquote><a href="/songs/katyusha">Katyusha</a></li><li><blockquote>The bitter divisions of the occupation fed directly into the <b>Greek Civil War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hellenic-state-1941-1944">Hellenic State (1941-1944)</a></li></ul></section>
